00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:22.340
Imagine you bought XIU last week when the TSX dipped on earnings misses and your order filled at $38.50. The superficial loss rule resets your adjusted cost base if you repurchase an identical security within 30 days, turning a planned tax saving into a disallowed loss.

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:35.900
The mechanism works like this. Sell the position at a loss, buy a similar but not identical ETF such as XIC, wait the full 30 days, then repurchase the original if desired.

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:49.280
Most retail investors do not realize the T5008 slip reports every disposition to the CRA, so tracking adjusted cost base manually prevents double counting disallowed losses.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:59.660
The capital gains inclusion rate change makes harvesting even more valuable this year because only half of gains are taxable while losses offset at the same rate.

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:10.920
A concrete example shows an investor selling 200 shares of XIU at a $4,200 loss, buying XIC for the 30-day window, then switching back.

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:26.740
The $4,200 loss can offset future capital gains even after the inclusion rate adjustment. Professionals always verify the exact 30-day window using trade settlement dates rather than trade dates to avoid accidental violations.

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:34.820
Assuming any sale automatically creates a usable loss ignores the 30-day rule and leads to denied claims on review.

